  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x+1)^{2}+(y+6)^{2}=16?$",
  "worked_solution": "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(y+6)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (x-(-1))^{2}+(y-(-6))^{2} & ={4}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=-1,$ $b=-6,$ and $r=4.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(-1,-6)$ and its radius is $4.$",
